  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures \nand lower humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes will track over the \nMid-Atlantic through Monday. This will bring a change in \nairmass with notably lower dew points. Highs in the low 80s for \nmost today and Monday. Overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid- \nAtlantic on Tuesday as upper level ridging builds over the\nsoutheast CONUS. Temperatures likely begin to creep up again \nduring this time, accompanied by increasing humidity as mid-\nlevel dry air tracks south of the region with more humid air \ncoming in from the north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid \nweek.\n\nUpper-level ridging over the Appalachians starts to flatten into \nzonal flow beginning Wed. As this occurs, a trough forming over\nQuebec will begin to track east across the northeast CONUS, \nwith a stemming cold front moving across the East Coast by\nThursday. Temps gradually increase during this time, along with\nincreasing humidity ahead of the front. By Thursday, temps \npotentially reach the lower 90s across the metros. \n\nAs this frontal boundary tracks through the Mid-Atlantic, additional \nshower & thunderstorm activity is possible across the region \nbeginning Wed evening and into Thursday. As of the 00Z model \nruns, the GFS and ECMWF both indicate a Thu afternoon time of \narrival for most precip, but the exact coverage and nature of \nprecip remains uncertain. Some diurnal thunderstorm development\nremains possible during this period given the increasing \ninstability in the atmosphere by this time.\n\nAs this cold front transitions offshore towards the end of next week,\ntemps drop again & conditions become much drier heading into \nthe weekend as high pressure moves in overhead.",
